  "prompt_text": "The figure is a conceptual overview of carbon dioxide capture, transport, and geological storage on land and offshore. It shows CO₂ coming either directly from an industrial emission source or from direct air capture (DAC), then being routed by pipelines or ships to places where it can be injected underground.\n\nOn land, CO₂ is sent to an injection well and pumped into subsurface rock formations, where it appears to spread through the reservoir as dissolved CO₂. Water movement is also shown, suggesting interaction between injected CO₂ and formation fluids. A nearby monitoring well indicates that stored CO₂ is tracked after injection. Another pathway highlights mineral storage, where dissolved CO₂ reacts underground to form carbonate minerals.\n\nOffshore, the same general strategy is extended beneath the seabed. CO₂ is transported by pipeline from shore or by cargo ship, then transferred to offshore injection rigs. The CO₂ is injected below the seafloor into deep geological formations, again with labels indicating dissolved CO₂ in the subsurface. Seawater is shown above these formations, emphasizing that storage occurs beneath the ocean floor rather than in open water.\n\nOverall, the image compares several linked CCS options: capture, transport, subsurface injection, monitoring, and long-term trapping in rock or minerals.",
